import System.IO.Console;
program AgentsExe;

// FIVE AXES, NOT A LABEL, and each axis has a customer already: who leaves, who talks, who hoards,
// who explores, who rises.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A LABEL. The nation model's §4 wants temperament to DESYNCHRONISE the population --
// so that a hundred agents in one city do not all decide the same thing on the same tick. A label
// is N buckets, and every agent in a bucket shares the bucket's threshold, which is the shared
// threshold problem wearing a different hat. Five bytes of continuous axes give every agent its own
// number for every question.
//
// Each is 0..255 and read as 0..1. A byte and not a float because there are going to be a great many
// agents and this is five fields of every one of them; nothing here needs more than one part in two
// hundred and fifty-six.
//
// `ubyte` AND NOT `byte`, and the layout said `byte`. A `byte` is signed -- it holds -128..127 --
// so an axis at 200 reads back as -56, and a bold agent becomes the most timid one in the world.
// This is the same trap the fauna layout fell into with `int state : 3` for eight states (ledger
// S1-1), in a document whose every other number had been measured. The range was stated correctly
// and the type could not hold it.
//
// FIXED AT BIRTH: the mean of the parents on each axis with a deviation, saturated at the bounds --
// which is a `Gauge` doing the saturating, so a child of two bold parents cannot come out bolder
// than the scale allows.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace People {

        public struct Temperament {
            public mutable ubyte boldness;      // who leaves: goes far from home, takes the risk
            public mutable ubyte sociability;   // who talks: seeks others, makes friends, courts
            public mutable ubyte greed;         // who hoards: keeps rather than gives, wants more
            public mutable ubyte curiosity;     // who explores: goes to see, learns, prospects
            public mutable ubyte ambition;      // who rises: seeks the post, the rank, the office
        }
    }
}
